{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_ubwmoz67ipzvo5coqv7eowkwtulihsohh7vp7op7hbqtw5bmvk6q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The build passes. Let me take a closer look at the get_record signature - I declared it with `&RM_TableData` but used unsafe to mutate. Let me also double check `pin_page` re-syncs the data correctly. Looking at my buffer_mgr.rs `pin_page`:\n\nThe issue: I sync `page.data` back to `bp.pagedata` at the start of pin_page when `page.data` is non-empty AND `page.page_num != NO_PAGE`. But that means that `page.data` might be from a different pinned page that we already synced via unpin_page. In that case, syncing would overwrite the pre-pinned page.\n\nActually, looking more carefully: in the C code, the pagehandler shares pointers - `page->data` is a pointer into `pagedata`. So mutations via `page->data` are seen directly. In my Rust impl, I clone the data into the page handle. So I need to sync back on unpin.\n\nBut what about when pin_page is called with a page that's already pinned in the same handler - this could result in the data being double-synced (the page handler still contains the previous page's data). Let me revisit this.\n\nActually the C code's behavior is: `page->data` is a raw pointer into the buffer pool's `pagedata`. So when pinPage is called again, `page->data` is overwritten with a pointer into a different region. There's no separate sync needed.\n\nIn my Rust code, I clone data on pin and sync back on unpin. The \"sync at start of pin\" is only needed if the previous page wasn't unpinned. The C code's flow always pins after unpinning, so my sync-on-unpin should be sufficient. Let me remove the sync in pin_page to avoid potential issues:\n\nActually wait - looking at the C code, in `forcePage` and `pinPage` cases where it's already pinned (case 1 - \"page already in pool\"), it just sets `page->data = &buffer_pool->pagedata[memory_address * PAGE_SIZE]`. The